From: Emre Yavuzalp <emreyavuzalp@gmail.com>
Subject: econet-eth: TB_CFG bit16 is entry-size select, not scan mode - fix 80B mode

Root cause of "BIND committed but the engine never hits it" (crsn stuck at
0d/0e/0f, 16 always 0): the FOE entry STRIDE the engine walks did not match
the 80-byte layout the driver writes.

Live proof (patch 149 BINDDBG, 8/8 dumps): the engine's own learned UNBIND
entries always start at 64-byte-aligned table offsets, i.e. the engine was
walking the table with a 64-byte stride while the driver reads/writes 80-byte
slots. Every driver BIND landed at bytes the engine never searches.

Vendor hw_nat.ko (EN751221-Linux26 SDK, chip check reads 0xbfb00064; live
value on the Archer C5v is 0x00070000) programs the entry size for chip
families 0x0007/0x0008 as a TWO-bit selection spread over TB_CFG bit16 and
bit3 (RegModifyBits sequences in PpeInitMod, gPpeType 1/2/3 with
gPpeFoeSize 32/64/80):

    gPpeType  size  bit16  bit3
       1      32B     1     0
       2      64B     0     0
       3      80B     0     1

Our init wrote bit3=1 (ENTRY_80B, correct) but ALSO bit16=1 via
FIELD_PREP(SCAN_MODE[17:16], 1) copied from the mtk netsys register map.
On EN751221 bit16 is NOT a scan-mode field - it is the other half of the
entry-size select. bit16=1 + bit3=1 is an illegal combination which the
engine resolved to a 64-byte stride.

Drop the bogus SCAN_MODE field so bit16 stays 0: with bit16=0/bit3=1 the
engine walks genuine 80-byte entries, aligned with the driver's layout,
hash slot computation and bind writes.

--- a/econet_ppe.c
+++ b/econet_ppe.c
@@ -432,10 +432,15 @@ void en75_ppe_start(struct en75_ppe *ppe
 	      FIELD_PREP(EN75_PPE_TB_CFG_KEEPALIVE,
 			 EN75_PPE_KEEPALIVE_DISABLE) |
 	      FIELD_PREP(EN75_PPE_TB_CFG_HASH_MODE, 1) |
-	      FIELD_PREP(EN75_PPE_TB_CFG_SCAN_MODE,
-			 EN75_PPE_SCAN_MODE_CHECK_AGE) |
+	      /* Do NOT set bit16: unlike mtk netsys there is no SCAN_MODE
+	       * field at [17:16] on EN751221. Vendor hw_nat programs the FOE
+	       * entry size as bit16/bit3 = 1/0 (32B), 0/0 (64B), 0/1 (80B).
+	       * Setting bit16 together with ENTRY_80B made the engine walk the
+	       * table with a 64-byte stride while the driver wrote 80-byte
+	       * slots, so driver BINDs were never hit (only 0d/0e/0f, no 16).
+	       */
 	      FIELD_PREP(EN75_PPE_TB_CFG_ENTRY_NUM, EN75_PPE_ENTRIES_SHIFT) |
 	      EN75_PPE_TB_CFG_ENTRY_80B;
 	ppe_w32(ppe, EN75_PPE_TB_CFG, val);

 	ppe_w32(ppe, EN75_PPE_IP_PROTO_CHK,
